algorithm--a set of steps or instructions for completing a task

when running into problems when coding it's useful to know of different algorithms so you don't waste time; solutions to most of the issues you're facing have been solved before

it's important to know what an algorithm does and when to apply it

algorithmic thinking--solving problems with algorithms

when solving a problem it's important to clearly define what the problem set is and, specify what values counts as inputs

with any given problem there are many ways to solve a problem, you need to find the best solution for your context

ex : the time that social media uses to look up a user matters

search algorithms
linear, simple, or sequential search
binary search : a sorted array is repeatedly divided in half until a solution is found

algorithms must have a clear problem statement, input, and output

algorithms must contain a specific number of distinct steps in a particular order

algorithms should produce a result

algorithms should actually complete and cannot take and infinite amount of time

when dealing with a large problem, break down the problem into smaller problems each defined in terms of an input and output

correctness--an algorithm is correct if it receives valid input, terminates, and always returns the correct output

efficiency--comes in two measures of time complexity defined in running time and space complexity (ex: memory) measured in ...

we can measure the efficiency of an algorithm in the best case scenario, average case, and worse case scenario

worst case scenarios are used as a benchmark of performance

when a computer executes a binary search it returns a position in a data list rather than the item in the data list itself

the growth rate, or order of growth--rates how efficient in time and space an algorithm is based on how large n amount of options grows

big O

a theoretical definition of the complexity of an algorithm as a function of the size

a variable, notated O(n), O--order of magnitude of complexity

n is size, big O is a function of size

the upper bound of the algorithm, big O measures the worst case scenario

relative to other algorithms

linear search has a time complexity of O(n)

binary search has a time complexity of O(log n)

algorithms don't necessarily have just one measure of complexity

when O(1) the amount of time for an algorithm to find a solution is constant despite the number of options



